# PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy

# This file is a helper/template for the Xcode Privacy Manifest required by Apple (2024+).
# Xcode expects a Privacy Manifest resource in the project. The safest way is to add it
# through Xcode: Project -> Target -> Signing & Capabilities -> + Capability -> Privacy Manifest
# or add a new "Privacy Manifest" file. Use the snippet below as the manifest contents / reasons.

# Guidance:
# - The project uses the following APIs that Apple considers "required reason" items:
#   • UserDefaults (@AppStorage and manual writes) — used to store user preferences and small app state
#   • FileManager.urls(for:in:) — used for cache/temporary files (image cache, downloads)
#   • URLSession — network requests for fetching movie data, posters and syncing reviews
#   • System boot time / uptime — may be accessed by third-party SDKs (e.g. Firebase) for analytics
# - Add the manifest via Xcode so it is included in the build. The SDKs (Firebase/GoogleSignIn)
#   may also include their own manifest entries; keep those and add app-specific entries here.

# Example privacy manifest entries (human-readable). Paste or create equivalent entries in Xcode.

# 1) User Defaults / App Storage
# Identifier: com.apple.developer.user-defaults
# Title: Local preferences and app settings
# Purpose: Required to store user preferences and small app state (e.g. language choice, UI preferences,
# review composer drafts) so the app can preserve user settings across launches and provide a better UX.

# 2) File system (Cache / Temporary files)
# Identifier: com.apple.developer.file-system-access
# Title: Cache and temporary storage
# Purpose: Required to persist cached images and temporary downloads to improve performance and offline
# experience (e.g. movie posters, temporary downloads). Files are stored in app cache directories.

# 3) Network (URLSession)
# Identifier: com.apple.developer.networking
# Title: Network access to fetch content
# Purpose: Required to fetch movie metadata, posters, and to synchronize user reviews and ratings with backend
# services (Firebase / custom APIs) using secure network requests.

# 4) System boot time (uptime)
# Identifier: com.apple.developer.device-boot-info
# Title: System boot time accessed by SDKs
# Purpose: Third-party SDKs (e.g. Firebase) may read system boot time to improve analytics or background
# sync calculations. The app itself does not directly expose boot time to the user.

# --- Sample JSON manifest snippet (for reference only) ---
# Xcode's Privacy Manifest UI will create a .xcprivacy plist; below is a representative JSON-like
# example used as guidance. Do NOT drop this raw JSON into the project unless you know Xcode will
# accept it. Instead copy the Titles/Purposes into Xcode UI when creating the manifest.

# {
#   "privacy": [
#     {
#       "identifier": "com.apple.developer.user-defaults",
#       "title": "Local preferences and app settings",
#       "purpose": "Required to store user preferences and app settings (e.g. language, drafts) to preserve state across launches."
#     },
#     {
#       "identifier": "com.apple.developer.file-system-access",
#       "title": "Cache and temporary storage",
#       "purpose": "Required to persist cached images and temporary downloads to improve offline experience and performance."
#     },
#     {
#       "identifier": "com.apple.developer.networking",
#       "title": "Network access to fetch content",
#       "purpose": "Required to fetch movie data, posters, and to synchronize user reviews and ratings with backend services using secure network requests."
#     },
#     {
#       "identifier": "com.apple.developer.device-boot-info",
#       "title": "System boot time accessed by SDKs",
#       "purpose": "Third-party SDKs (e.g. Firebase) may read system boot time for analytics/background sync."
#     }
#   ]
# }

# Next steps after adding this file:
# 1) Open Xcode, add a Privacy Manifest capability or add a new Privacy Manifest file to the target.
# 2) Use the titles/purposes above when populating the manifest entries in Xcode.
# 3) Archive and validate before uploading to App Store Connect; Xcode will surface missing manifest issues
#    during the upload/validation step.

# App Store Connect — App Privacy mapping (what the app collects)
#
# The App Privacy questionnaire in App Store Connect should reflect the following data collection
# based on current code and SDK usage. Please mark these appropriately when you submit:
#
# 1) Email Address
#    - Collected: Yes (Firebase Authentication email used for sign-in).
#    - Linked to identity: Yes (used for account and login).
#    - Purpose: Authentication, account management, password reset.
#
# 2) Name
#    - Collected: Yes (displayName from sign-in providers or user profile).
#    - Linked to identity: Yes.
#    - Purpose: Profile display, personalization.
#
# 3) User ID
#    - Collected: Yes (Firebase UID used as user identifier and for reviews ownership).
#    - Linked to identity: Yes.
#    - Purpose: Associate content (reviews) and user data.
#
# 4) Photos
#    - Collected: Yes (user-selected avatar uploaded via PhotosPicker / image upload flows).
#    - Linked to identity: Yes (avatar associated with user profile in Firestore).
#    - Purpose: User profile picture.
#
# 5) Diagnostic Data
#    - Collected: Possibly No by app code (no explicit Firebase Analytics import found).
#    - Action: If you include Firebase Analytics or Crashlytics in your build, mark Diagnostic Data = Yes.
#      Otherwise mark No. Double-check your Package/Pods to confirm Analytics is not bundled.
#
# 6) User Content
#    - Collected: Yes (reviews, comments, replies authored by users are stored in Firestore under reviews_v2/*).
#    - Linked to identity: Yes (content is linked to userId unless anonymised).
#    - Purpose: Display and moderation of user-generated reviews/comments.
